<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- Copyright SDMX 2025 - http://www.sdmx.org -->
<xs:schema targetNamespace="http://www.sdmx.org/resources/sdmxml/schemas/v3_2/structure" 
			xmlns="http://www.sdmx.org/resources/sdmxml/schemas/v3_2/structure" 
			xmlns:xs="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema"
			xmlns:common="http://www.sdmx.org/resources/sdmxml/schemas/v3_2/common"
			xmlns:structure="http://www.sdmx.org/resources/sdmxml/schemas/v3_2/structure" 
			elementFormDefault="qualified">
			
	<xs:import namespace="http://www.sdmx.org/resources/sdmxml/schemas/v3_2/common" schemaLocation="SDMXCommon.xsd"/>
	<xs:include schemaLocation="SDMXStructureBase.xsd"/>

	<xs:annotation>
		<xs:appinfo>SDMX Transformation Structure Module</xs:appinfo>
		<xs:documentation>The transformation structure module defines the structure of transformation scheme and transformation constructs.</xs:documentation>
	</xs:annotation>
	
	<xs:complexType name="VtlDefinitionSchemeType" abstract="true">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>VtlDefinitionSchemeType is an abstract extension of the ItemSchemeType for VTL schemes.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:extension base="ItemSchemeType">
				<xs:attribute name="vtlVersion" type="xs:string" use="required">
					<xs:annotation>
						<xs:documentation>Identifies the VTL version to which the items in the defined scheme comply (e.g. 2.2), which determines the syntax used in their definitions. It is mandatory. Note that definition schemes can only reference definition schemes using the same VTL version.</xs:documentation>
					</xs:annotation>
				</xs:attribute>
			</xs:extension>
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>
	
	<xs:complexType name="TransformationSchemeBaseType" abstract="true">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>TransformationSchemeBaseType is an abstract base type for the TransformationSchemeType. It restricts the item types to be only transformations.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:restriction base="VtlDefinitionSchemeType">
				<xs:sequence>
					<xs:element ref="common:Annotations" minOccurs="0"/>
					<xs:element ref="common:Link" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
					<xs:element ref="common:Name" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
					<xs:element ref="common:Description" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
					<xs:sequence minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
						<xs:element ref="Transformation"/>
					</xs:sequence>
				</xs:sequence>
				<xs:attribute name="urn" type="common:TransformationSchemeUrnType" use="optional"/>
			</xs:restriction>
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>
	
	<xs:complexType name="TransformationSchemeType">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>TransformationSchemeType describes the structure of a transformation scheme. A transformation scheme contains a set of transformations to be executed together (in the same run). It can contain any number of transformations that produce any number of results. It references all the other schemes used by the VTL program (the VTL mapping scheme, the ruleset schemes and the user defined operator schemes); the semantic analysis and the execution of the program detect any missing definition.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:extension base="TransformationSchemeBaseType">
				<xs:sequence>
					<xs:element name="VtlMappingScheme" type="common:VtlMappingSchemeReferenceType" minOccurs="0">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>References the VTL mapping scheme which defines the aliases of the SDMX artefacts used in the transformations, rulesets and user defined operators of the transformation scheme. As the VTL is strongly typed and has no declarations of variables, its mappings provide the definitions of the VTL Data Sets and Value Domains used by the VTL program; therefore, a transformation scheme that references SDMX artefacts is complete only together with its VTL mapping scheme.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
					<xs:element name="NamePersonalisationScheme" type="common:NamePersonalisationSchemeReferenceType" minOccurs="0">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>Deprecated. The name personalisation schemes are deprecated in SDMX 3.2: the names assigned by default by the VTL operators (e.g. bool_var, errorcode) can be changed in the VTL program, for example through the rename clause. References a name personalisation scheme, which defines the overriding of some standard VTL names (to be assigned to some measures and/or attributes of the data structure) with some corresponding personalised names. This must be used if transformations within a transformation scheme personalise standard names. All personalisations must be defined in the referenced scheme.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
					<xs:element name="CustomTypeScheme" type="common:CustomTypeSchemeReferenceType" minOccurs="0">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>Deprecated. The custom type schemes are deprecated in SDMX 3.2: the default conversions between VTL and SDMX data types apply, unless overridden by the representation defined in the data structure definition of the referenced SDMX artefact; the VTL NULLs become missing values in SDMX, the VTL literals are written in the default format, and explicit type conversions are expressed in the VTL program through the cast operator. References a custom type scheme which defines custom conversions of VTL scalar types to SDMX data types. This must be used if custom type conversions are used in the transformations defined in a transformation scheme. All custom conversions must be defined in the referenced scheme.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
					<xs:element name="RulesetScheme" type="common:RulesetSchemeReferenceType" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>References a ruleset scheme that defines rulesets used by the VTL program, i.e. rulesets invoked by VTL operators or, from VTL 2.2, viral propagations. The ruleset schemes referenced by a transformation scheme must contain all the rulesets used by its transformations, also indirectly (e.g. the rulesets invoked by a user defined operator), and all the viral propagations to be applied.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
					<xs:element name="UserDefinedOperatorScheme" type="common:UserDefinedOperatorSchemeReferenceType" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>References a user defined operator scheme that defines one or more user defined operators used by the transformations of the transformation scheme. The user defined operator schemes referenced by a transformation scheme must contain all the user defined operators used by its transformations, also indirectly (e.g. the operators invoked by other user defined operators).</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
				</xs:sequence>
			</xs:extension>
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>

	<xs:element name="Transformation" type="TransformationType" substitutionGroup="Item">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Transformation describes the details of a single transformation within a transformation scheme.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>

	<xs:complexType name="TransformationBaseType" abstract="true">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>TransformationBaseType defines the base refinement of the TransformationType. Its purpose is to restrict the urn attribute.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:restriction base="UnnestedItemType">
				<xs:sequence>
					<xs:element ref="common:Annotations" minOccurs="0"/>
					<xs:element ref="common:Link" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
					<xs:element ref="common:Name" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
					<xs:element ref="common:Description" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
				</xs:sequence>
				<xs:attribute name="urn" type="common:TransformationUrnType" use="optional"/>
			</xs:restriction>
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>

	<xs:complexType name="TransformationType">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>TransformationType defines the structure of a transformation. A transformation is an assignment of a VTL expression to a specific result.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:extension base="TransformationBaseType">
				<xs:sequence>
					<xs:element name="Expression" type="xs:string">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>The right-hand side of the VTL statement. This is the expression that is executed for this transformation. It includes references to operands and other artefacts. The SDMX artefacts are referenced through the aliases (vtlAlias) defined in the VTL mapping scheme of the transformation scheme. The references to SDMX artefacts through their URN or the abbreviated URN (see the SDMX Technical Notes, "References to SDMX artefacts from VTL statements") are deprecated.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
					<xs:element name="Result" type="xs:string">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>The left-hand side of the VTL statement. This identifies the result artefact, which may be used in subsequent transformations. If the result is an output of the VTL program whose data are converted into SDMX, it is the vtlAlias of a VtlDatasetMapping of the VTL mapping scheme whose isInput is false, and the transformation must be persistent. The references through the URN or the abbreviated URN are deprecated.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
				</xs:sequence>
				<xs:attribute name="isPersistent" type="xs:boolean" use="required">
					<xs:annotation>
						<xs:documentation>Indicates if the result is permanently stored. A persistent result (value of true) can be used by transformation defined in other transformation schemes, but a non-persistent result (value of false) can only be used by transformations within the same transformation scheme.</xs:documentation>
					</xs:annotation>
				</xs:attribute>
			</xs:extension>
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>

	<xs:complexType name="VtlMappingSchemeBaseType" abstract="true">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>VtlMappingSchemeBaseType is an abstract base type for the VtlMappingSchemeType. It restricts the urn attribute and removes the generic items, which the VtlMappingSchemeType replaces with the VTL mappings.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:restriction base="ItemSchemeType">
				<xs:sequence>
					<xs:element ref="common:Annotations" minOccurs="0"/>
					<xs:element ref="common:Link" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
					<xs:element ref="common:Name" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
					<xs:element ref="common:Description" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
				</xs:sequence>
				<xs:attribute name="urn" type="common:VtlMappingSchemeUrnType" use="optional"/>
			</xs:restriction>
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>

	<xs:complexType name="VtlMappingSchemeType">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>VtlMappingSchemeType defines a set of mappings between SDMX and VTL. The mappings define the VTL Data Sets and Value Domains used by the transformations, rulesets and user defined operators of a transformation scheme: a VtlDatasetMapping maps a VTL Data Set to a Data Structure Definition, Dataflow or Provision Agreement, and a VtlCodelistMapping maps a VTL Value Domain to a Codelist. The VtlMapping is the deprecated encoding of the mappings used in SDMX 3.0 and 3.1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:extension base="VtlMappingSchemeBaseType">
				<xs:choice minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
					<xs:element ref="VtlDatasetMapping"/>
					<xs:element ref="VtlCodelistMapping"/>
					<xs:element ref="VtlMapping"/>
				</xs:choice>
			</xs:extension>
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>

	<xs:element name="VtlDatasetMapping" type="VtlDatasetMappingType" substitutionGroup="Item">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>VtlDatasetMapping details a mapping between a VTL Data Set and the SDMX Data Structure Definition, Dataflow or Provision Agreement that defines it.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>

	<xs:complexType name="VtlDatasetMappingBaseType" abstract="true">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>VtlDatasetMappingBaseType defines the base refinement of the VtlDatasetMappingType. Its purpose is to restrict the urn attribute.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:restriction base="UnnestedItemType">
				<xs:sequence>
					<xs:element ref="common:Annotations" minOccurs="0"/>
					<xs:element ref="common:Link" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
					<xs:element ref="common:Name" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
					<xs:element ref="common:Description" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
				</xs:sequence>
				<xs:attribute name="urn" type="common:VtlDatasetMappingUrnType" use="optional"/>
			</xs:restriction>
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>

	<xs:complexType name="VtlDatasetMappingType">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>VtlDatasetMappingType defines the structure of a mapping between a VTL Data Set, named by its alias, and an SDMX Data Structure Definition, Dataflow or Provision Agreement. As the VTL is strongly typed and has no declarations of variables, the mapping is the definition of the VTL Data Set used by the transformations, rulesets and user defined operators of a transformation scheme. The data structure of the VTL Data Set corresponds to the Data Structure Definition (referenced directly, or used by the Dataflow, referenced directly or through the Provision Agreement) through the Basic mapping, which maps the Dimensions and the TimeDimension to VTL Identifiers, the Measures to VTL Measures and the DataAttributes to VTL Attributes, and vice-versa. The same SDMX artefact can be referenced by several VTL dataset mappings, with distinct aliases.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:extension base="VtlDatasetMappingBaseType">
				<xs:choice>
					<xs:element name="DataStructure" type="common:DataStructureReferenceType">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>A reference to the SDMX Data Structure Definition to which the VTL Data Set is mapped.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
					<xs:element name="Dataflow" type="common:DataflowReferenceType">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>A reference to the SDMX Dataflow to which the VTL Data Set is mapped.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
					<xs:element name="ProvisionAgreement" type="common:ProvisionAgreementReferenceType">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>A reference to the SDMX Provision Agreement to which the VTL Data Set is mapped.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
				</xs:choice>
				<xs:attribute name="vtlAlias" type="VtlAliasType" use="required">
					<xs:annotation>
						<xs:documentation>The alias used in the VTL transformations, rulesets and user defined operators to refer to the VTL Data Set. It cannot be empty, as it identifies the VTL Data Set defined by the mapping; it is a VTL name given without single quotes (see VtlAliasType) and must be unique within the VTL mapping scheme.</xs:documentation>
					</xs:annotation>
				</xs:attribute>
				<xs:attribute name="isInput" type="xs:boolean" use="required">
					<xs:annotation>
						<xs:documentation>Indicates whether the VTL Data Set is an input of the VTL program (true), whose definition and data are obtained from SDMX, or an output of the VTL program (false), whose data are converted into SDMX. An output must be persistent, i.e. the vtlAlias must be the result of a transformation whose isPersistent is true. An SDMX artefact that is both read and written by the VTL program is mapped twice, with distinct aliases.</xs:documentation>
					</xs:annotation>
				</xs:attribute>
			</xs:extension>
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>

	<xs:element name="VtlCodelistMapping" type="VtlCodelistMappingType" substitutionGroup="Item">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>VtlCodelistMapping details a mapping between a VTL Value Domain and the SDMX Codelist whose codes are its values.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>

	<xs:complexType name="VtlCodelistMappingBaseType" abstract="true">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>VtlCodelistMappingBaseType defines the base refinement of the VtlCodelistMappingType. Its purpose is to restrict the urn attribute.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:restriction base="UnnestedItemType">
				<xs:sequence>
					<xs:element ref="common:Annotations" minOccurs="0"/>
					<xs:element ref="common:Link" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
					<xs:element ref="common:Name" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
					<xs:element ref="common:Description" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
				</xs:sequence>
				<xs:attribute name="urn" type="common:VtlCodelistMappingUrnType" use="optional"/>
			</xs:restriction>
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>

	<xs:complexType name="VtlCodelistMappingType">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>VtlCodelistMappingType defines the structure of a mapping between a VTL Value Domain, named by its alias, and an SDMX Codelist, whose codes are the values of the Value Domain. As the VTL programs do not produce Value Domains, the mapping applies only to inputs.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:extension base="VtlCodelistMappingBaseType">
				<xs:sequence>
					<xs:element name="Codelist" type="common:CodelistReferenceType">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>A reference to the SDMX Codelist whose codes are the values of the VTL Value Domain.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
				</xs:sequence>
				<xs:attribute name="vtlAlias" type="VtlAliasType" use="required">
					<xs:annotation>
						<xs:documentation>The alias used in the VTL transformations, rulesets and user defined operators to refer to the VTL Value Domain. It cannot be empty, as it identifies the VTL Value Domain defined by the mapping; it is a VTL name given without single quotes (see VtlAliasType) and must be unique within the VTL mapping scheme.</xs:documentation>
					</xs:annotation>
				</xs:attribute>
			</xs:extension>
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>

	<xs:simpleType name="VtlAliasType">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>A simple type for the alias used in VTL to refer to a mapped SDMX artefact. It is a name compliant with the VTL naming rules, given without the single quotes that can enclose it in the VTL statements: a name that coincides with a VTL keyword (e.g. diff) or contains characters not allowed in the VTL regular names (e.g. 1ab-cde) is written between single quotes in the VTL statements ('diff'), while a regular name (e.g. ds_a) can be written with or without single quotes (ds_a or 'ds_a'). As a quoted and an unquoted name denote the same artefact, the VTL engines identify the mapped VTL Data Set or Value Domain in both cases. The alias cannot contain whitespace or single quotes and is at most 128 characters long.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:restriction base="xs:string">
			<xs:pattern value="[^\s']+"/>
			<xs:maxLength value="128"/>
		</xs:restriction>
	</xs:simpleType>

	<xs:element name="VtlMapping" type="VtlMappingType" substitutionGroup="Item">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Deprecated. Replaced by VtlDatasetMapping and VtlCodelistMapping. VtlMapping details a mapping between SDMX and VTL transformation.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>

	<xs:complexType name="VtlMappingBaseType" abstract="true">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Deprecated. Replaced by VtlDatasetMappingBaseType and VtlCodelistMappingBaseType. VtlMappingBaseType defines the base refinement of the VtlMappingType. Its purpose is to restrict the urn attribute.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:restriction base="UnnestedItemType">
				<xs:sequence>
					<xs:element ref="common:Annotations" minOccurs="0"/>
					<xs:element ref="common:Link" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
					<xs:element ref="common:Name" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
					<xs:element ref="common:Description" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
				</xs:sequence>
				<xs:attribute name="urn" type="common:VtlMappingUrnType" use="optional"/>
			</xs:restriction>
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>

	<xs:complexType name="VtlMappingType">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Deprecated. Replaced by VtlDatasetMappingType and VtlCodelistMappingType, as the VtlDataflowMapping and the VtlConceptMapping are deprecated and the Basic mapping is the only mapping method (see the SDMX Technical Notes, "Conversion of definitions based on deprecated constructs"). VtlMappingType defines the structure of a single mapping between the reference to a SDMX artefact made from VTL transformations, rulesets, user defined operators and the corresponding SDMX structure identifier. These are used to assign an alternative names to SDMX Dataflows, Codelists, Concept Schemes, or Concepts. Although are distinct sub-classes in the Information Model, this structure serves to express them all. The references SDMX artefact serves to distinguish which type of sub-class (VtlDatflowMapping or VtlCodelistMapping, or VtlConceptMapping) is being described. When this is used to assign an alias for a SDMX Dataflow, this can also be used to indicate the methods used to convert the data structure from SDMX to VTL and vice-versa. Finally, this can be used to override the default Basic mapping methods used for Dataflows by utilizing the GenericDataflow element in place of a reference to a specific Dataflow.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:extension base="VtlMappingBaseType">
				<xs:sequence>
					<xs:choice>
						<xs:sequence>
							<xs:choice>
								<xs:element name="Dataflow" type="common:DataflowReferenceType">
									<xs:annotation>
										<xs:documentation>Deprecated. A VTL Data Set is mapped to a Dataflow through a VtlDatasetMapping. A reference to the SDMX Dataflow that the mapping is defined for. This is used to create a VtlDataflowMapping. In this case, it is possible to specify the mapping details to and from VTL.</xs:documentation>
									</xs:annotation>
								</xs:element>
								<xs:element name="GenericDataflow" type="common:EmptyType">
									<xs:annotation>
										<xs:documentation>Deprecated. The Basic mapping is the only mapping method, therefore no default mapping methods can be declared. Indicates that the ToVtlMapping and FromVtlMapping are the default mapping methods to be applied, overriding the Basic methods if necessary. The mapping methods in this case would be applied to any dataflows that are not explicitly defined in other VtlMappings.</xs:documentation>
									</xs:annotation>
								</xs:element>
							</xs:choice>
							<xs:element name="ToVtlMapping" type="ToVtlMappingType" minOccurs="0">
								<xs:annotation>
									<xs:documentation>Deprecated. The Basic mapping is the only mapping method between SDMX and VTL data structures; any restructuring of the data is performed in the VTL program through the VTL operators. Describes the mapping from a SDMX dataflow to a VTL data structure.</xs:documentation>
								</xs:annotation>
							</xs:element>
							<xs:element name="FromVtlMapping" type="FromVtlMappingType" minOccurs="0">
								<xs:annotation>
									<xs:documentation>Deprecated. The Basic mapping is the only mapping method between SDMX and VTL data structures; any restructuring of the data is performed in the VTL program through the VTL operators. Describes the mapping to a SDMX dataflow from a VTL data structure.</xs:documentation>
								</xs:annotation>
							</xs:element>
						</xs:sequence>
						<xs:element name="Codelist" type="common:CodelistReferenceType">
							<xs:annotation>
								<xs:documentation>Deprecated. A VTL Value Domain is mapped to a Codelist through the VtlCodelistMapping element. A reference to the SDMX Codelist that the mapping is defined for. This is used to create a VtlCodelistMapping.</xs:documentation>
							</xs:annotation>
						</xs:element>
						<xs:element name="Concept" type="common:ConceptReferenceType">
							<xs:annotation>
								<xs:documentation>Deprecated. The VTL Variables correspond to the homonymous components of the VTL Data Sets and do not need any mapping. A reference to the SDMX Concept that the mapping is defined for. This is used to create a VtlConceptMapping.</xs:documentation>
							</xs:annotation>
						</xs:element>
					</xs:choice>
				</xs:sequence>
				<xs:attribute name="alias" type="xs:string" use="required">
					<xs:annotation>
						<xs:documentation>Deprecated. Replaced by the vtlAlias of VtlDatasetMapping and VtlCodelistMapping; it represents the deprecated dataflowAlias, codelistAlias and conceptAlias. The alias used to refer to the reference SDMX artefact in the transformations. This must be unique within the mapping scheme in which it is defined.</xs:documentation>
					</xs:annotation>
				</xs:attribute>
			</xs:extension>
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>

	<xs:complexType name="ToVtlMappingType">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Deprecated. The Basic mapping is the only mapping method from SDMX to VTL; parts of the data are retrieved through the query parameters of a data extraction of a VTL execution and selected in the VTL program through the sub clause. ToVtlMappingType defines the mapping method and filter used when mapping from SDMX to VTL.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="ToVtlSubSpace" type="SpaceKeyType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Deprecated. The same SDMX artefact can be mapped to distinct input VTL Data Sets, each one fed with the desired part of the data through the query parameters of its data extraction, and the VTL program selects the parts through the sub clause. Identifies a sub space of the mapped dataflow that the mapping applies to. This is a collection of references to the dimensions that make up the space.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
		<xs:attribute name="method" type="ToVtlMappingMethodType" use="optional">
			<xs:annotation>
				<xs:documentation>Deprecated. The Basic mapping is the only mapping method. The mapping method used when mapping from SDMX to VTL. This is typically a StandardToVtlMappingMethodType, but can be any other value to allow for non-standard methods. The implied default is Basic.</xs:documentation>
			</xs:annotation>		
		</xs:attribute>
	</xs:complexType>

	<xs:simpleType name="StandardToVtlMappingMethodType">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Deprecated. The Basic mapping is the only mapping method. A simple type enumerating the standard mapping methods when converting from data structures from SDMX to VLT.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:restriction base="xs:string">
			<xs:enumeration value="Basic">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>The default mapping method. See Section 6 SDMX Standards ("SDMX Technical Notes"), 10.3.3.1 ("Basic Mapping").</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="Pivot">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Method for mapping multi-measure data. See Section 6 SDMX Standards ("SDMX Technical Notes"), 10.3.3.2 ("Pivot Mapping").</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="Basic-A2M">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>The basic mapping method, using attributes to measures. See Section 6 SDMX Standards ("SDMX Technical Notes"), 10.3.3.3 ("From SDMX DataAttributes to VTL Measures").</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="Pivot-A2M">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>The pivot mapping method, using attributes to measures. See Section 6 SDMX Standards ("SDMX Technical Notes"), 10.3.3.3 ("From SDMX DataAttributes to VTL Measures").</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
		</xs:restriction>
	</xs:simpleType>

	<xs:simpleType name="ToVtlMappingMethodType">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Deprecated. The Basic mapping is the only mapping method. A simple type that allows for a standard to VTL mapping method enumeration value or a string value for other mapping methods.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:union memberTypes="StandardToVtlMappingMethodType xs:string"/>
	</xs:simpleType>

	<xs:complexType name="FromVtlMappingType">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Deprecated. The Basic mapping is the only mapping method from VTL to SDMX; the VTL program restructures the data (e.g. through the calc clause and the union operator) and its result is mapped through a VtlDatasetMapping whose isInput is false. FromVtlMappingType defines the mapping method and filter used when mapping from VTL to SDMX.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="FromVtlSuperSpace" type="SpaceKeyType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Deprecated. The VTL program adds the mapping dimensions through the calc clause and combines the VTL Data Sets through the union operator. Identifies a super space of the mapped dataflow that the mapping applies to. This is a collection of references to the dimensions that make up the space.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
		<xs:attribute name="method" type="FromVtlMappingMethodType" use="optional">
			<xs:annotation>
				<xs:documentation>Deprecated. The Basic mapping is the only mapping method. The mapping method used when mapping from VTL to SDMX. This is typically a StandardFromVtlMappingMethodType, but can be any other value to allow for non-standard methods. The implied default is Basic for single-measure VTL data structures and Unpivot for multi-meausre VTL data structures.</xs:documentation>
			</xs:annotation>
		</xs:attribute>
	</xs:complexType>

	<xs:simpleType name="StandardFromVtlMappingMethodType">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Deprecated. The Basic mapping is the only mapping method. A simple type enumerating the standard mapping methods when converting from data structures from VTL to SDMX.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:restriction base="xs:string">
			<xs:enumeration value="Basic">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>The default mapping method, applicable only when the VLT data structure has just one measure component. See Section 6 SDMX Standards ("SDMX Technical Notes"), 10.3.4.1 ("Basic Mapping").</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="Unpivot">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>The mapping method to be used when the VTL data structure has more than one measure component. See Section 6 SDMX Standards ("SDMX Technical Notes"), 10.3.4.2 ("Unpivot Mapping").</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="M2A">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Mapping of multi-measure VTL where on measure is mapped to the SDMX primary measure and the remaining measures are mapped as data attributes. See Section 6 SDMX Standards ("SDMX Technical Notes"), 10.3.4.3 ("From VTL Measures to SDMX DataAttributes").</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
		</xs:restriction>
	</xs:simpleType>

	<xs:simpleType name="FromVtlMappingMethodType">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Deprecated. The Basic mapping is the only mapping method. A simple type that allows for standard from VTL mapping method enumeration value or a string value for other mapping methods.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:union memberTypes="StandardFromVtlMappingMethodType xs:string"/>
	</xs:simpleType>

	<xs:complexType name="SpaceKeyType">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Deprecated. Used only by the deprecated ToVtlSubSpace and FromVtlSuperSpace. SpaceKey defines the structure of a super- or sub- space for a SDMX Dataflow. It is a collection of references to the dimensions that make up the space.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="Key" type="common:SingleNCNameIDType" maxOccurs="unbounded">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>A reference to a dimension by its identifier.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>

	<xs:complexType name="VtlExecutionBaseType" abstract="true">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>VtlExecutionBaseType defines the base refinement of the VtlExecutionType. Its purpose is to restrict the urn attribute.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:restriction base="common:MaintainableType">
				<xs:sequence>
					<xs:element ref="common:Annotations" minOccurs="0"/>
					<xs:element ref="common:Link" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
					<xs:element ref="common:Name" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
					<xs:element ref="common:Description" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
				</xs:sequence>
				<xs:attribute name="urn" type="common:VtlExecutionUrnType" use="optional"/>
			</xs:restriction>
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>

	<xs:complexType name="VtlExecutionType">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>VtlExecutionType describes the structure of a VTL execution, which specifies how the data of the input VTL Data Sets of a transformation scheme are retrieved, so that the transformation scheme can be executed on the basis of its SDMX definition. A VTL execution is an execution configuration, maintained independently of the transformation scheme: several executions can reference the same transformation scheme, also one maintained by another organisation, and the sources of the data can change without a new version of the transformation scheme. It contains at most one data extraction for each input VTL Data Set; the data of the inputs without a data extraction are provided to the VTL engine at run time. As for the other maintainable artefacts, a VTL execution included as an external reference (isExternalReference="true") still contains the reference to its transformation scheme and at least one data extraction.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:extension base="VtlExecutionBaseType">
				<xs:sequence>
					<xs:element name="TransformationScheme" type="common:TransformationSchemeReferenceType">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>References the transformation scheme to be executed.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
					<xs:choice maxOccurs="unbounded">
						<xs:element name="QueryExtraction" type="QueryExtractionType">
							<xs:annotation>
								<xs:documentation>Retrieval of the data of an input VTL Data Set through a query to a web service compliant with the SDMX REST API.</xs:documentation>
							</xs:annotation>
							<xs:unique name="QueryExtraction_UniqueQueryParameter">
								<xs:selector xpath="structure:QueryParameter"/>
								<xs:field xpath="@name"/>
							</xs:unique>
						</xs:element>
						<xs:element name="UrlExtraction" type="UrlExtractionType">
							<xs:annotation>
								<xs:documentation>Retrieval of the data of an input VTL Data Set through a URL built from a template, for web services that are not fully compliant with the SDMX REST API.</xs:documentation>
							</xs:annotation>
							<xs:unique name="UrlExtraction_UniqueQueryParameter">
								<xs:selector xpath="structure:QueryParameter"/>
								<xs:field xpath="@name"/>
							</xs:unique>
						</xs:element>
					</xs:choice>
				</xs:sequence>
			</xs:extension>
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>

	<xs:complexType name="VtlDataExtractionType" abstract="true">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>VtlDataExtractionType is an abstract base type for the data extractions of a VTL execution. A data extraction specifies how to retrieve the data of an input VTL Data Set. It is not identifiable, as it is part of its VTL execution.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="DatasetMapping" type="common:VtlDatasetMappingReferenceType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>References the VTL dataset mapping of the input VTL Data Set whose data are retrieved. The mapping must belong to the VTL mapping scheme of the transformation scheme of the VTL execution, and its isInput must be true. A VTL execution has at most one data extraction for each VTL dataset mapping.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>

	<xs:complexType name="QueryExtractionType">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>QueryExtractionType describes the retrieval of the data of an input VTL Data Set through a query to a web service compliant with the SDMX REST API. The VTL engine builds the data query for the artefact referenced by the context or, if absent, by the VTL dataset mapping, according to the syntax of the version of the SDMX REST API, by using the query parameters: the parameter named key, if present, provides the key of the data query, while the other parameters are added as query parameters (e.g. startPeriod, or component filters such as c[TIME_PERIOD]).</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:extension base="VtlDataExtractionType">
				<xs:sequence>
					<xs:element name="EntryPoint" type="EntryPointType">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>The URL of the entry point of the SDMX REST web service (e.g. https://sdmx.example.org/rest). It cannot be empty.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
					<xs:element name="Context" type="common:DataflowOrProvisionAgreementReferenceType" minOccurs="0">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>References the dataflow or provision agreement whose data are queried, which must use the same data structure definition as the SDMX artefact referenced by the VTL dataset mapping (i.e. the data structure definition itself, or the one used by the dataflow, referenced directly or through the provision agreement). If not specified, the data of the SDMX artefact referenced by the VTL dataset mapping are queried.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
					<xs:element name="QueryParameter" type="QueryParameterType" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>A name-value pair used for building the query. The names are unique within the data extraction.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
				</xs:sequence>
				<xs:attribute name="sdmxApiVersion" type="SdmxApiVersionType" use="optional">
					<xs:annotation>
						<xs:documentation>The version of the SDMX REST API implemented by the entry point, which determines the syntax of the query. If not specified, the default version of the web service is assumed.</xs:documentation>
					</xs:annotation>
				</xs:attribute>
			</xs:extension>
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>

	<xs:complexType name="UrlExtractionType">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>UrlExtractionType describes the retrieval of the data of an input VTL Data Set through a URL built from a template, meant for web services that are not fully compliant with the SDMX REST API.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:extension base="VtlDataExtractionType">
				<xs:sequence>
					<xs:element name="UrlTemplate" type="UrlTemplateType">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>The complete URL of the query, whose tokens enclosed in curly braces (e.g. {startPeriod}) are replaced by the values of the query parameters having the same names. Each token must have a query parameter with the same name. Any part of the URL, including the entry point, can be a token. It cannot be empty.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
					<xs:element name="QueryParameter" type="QueryParameterType" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>A name-value pair providing the value of the token of the URL template having the same name. The names are unique within the data extraction.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
				</xs:sequence>
			</xs:extension>
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>

	<xs:complexType name="QueryParameterType">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>QueryParameterType describes a name-value pair used for retrieving the data of an input VTL Data Set. A fixed parameter must have a value, which is always used as it is. A non-fixed parameter can have a value, which is a default that can be overridden at run time; if it has no value, its value must be provided at run time. The values provided at run time are assigned to the input VTL Data Sets through their aliases. The credentials needed to access protected web services should not be put in the query parameters or in the URL template. An empty value (value="") is no value, the same as a null: it does not satisfy a fixed parameter, and a non-fixed parameter with an empty value has no default.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:attribute name="name" type="QueryParameterNameType" use="required">
			<xs:annotation>
				<xs:documentation>The name of the parameter (e.g. key, startPeriod, c[TIME_PERIOD]), unique within the data extraction. It cannot be empty or contain whitespace.</xs:documentation>
			</xs:annotation>
		</xs:attribute>
		<xs:attribute name="value" type="xs:string" use="optional">
			<xs:annotation>
				<xs:documentation>The value of the parameter: mandatory if the parameter is fixed, otherwise a default value that can be overridden at run time. An empty value is no value, the same as a null.</xs:documentation>
			</xs:annotation>
		</xs:attribute>
		<xs:attribute name="isFixed" type="xs:boolean" use="required">
			<xs:annotation>
				<xs:documentation>Indicates whether the value is fixed (true) or can be overridden at run time (false). A non-fixed parameter without a value must be given a value at run time.</xs:documentation>
			</xs:annotation>
		</xs:attribute>
	</xs:complexType>

	<xs:simpleType name="EntryPointType">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>A simple type for the URL of the entry point of a web service compliant with the SDMX REST API (e.g. https://sdmx.example.org/rest). It cannot be empty.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:restriction base="xs:anyURI">
			<xs:minLength value="1"/>
		</xs:restriction>
	</xs:simpleType>

	<xs:simpleType name="UrlTemplateType">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>A simple type for the template of the URL of a query, whose tokens enclosed in curly braces (e.g. {startPeriod}) are replaced by the values of the query parameters having the same names. It cannot be empty.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:restriction base="xs:string">
			<xs:minLength value="1"/>
		</xs:restriction>
	</xs:simpleType>

	<xs:simpleType name="QueryParameterNameType">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>A simple type for the name of a query parameter (e.g. key, startPeriod or c[TIME_PERIOD]). It cannot be empty or contain whitespace.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:restriction base="xs:string">
			<xs:pattern value="\S+"/>
		</xs:restriction>
	</xs:simpleType>

	<xs:simpleType name="SdmxApiVersionType">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>A simple type for the version of the SDMX REST API implemented by a web service. It is given by the code of a release of the SDMX REST API, written as major.minor, without the leading "v" and the patch number (e.g. 1.5, 2.1 or 2.2, from the releases v1.5.0, v2.1.0 and v2.2.2).</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:restriction base="xs:string">
			<xs:pattern value="(0|[1-9]\d*)\.(0|[1-9]\d*)"/>
		</xs:restriction>
	</xs:simpleType>

	<xs:complexType name="NamePersonalisationSchemeType">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Deprecated. The name personalisation schemes are deprecated in SDMX 3.2: the names assigned by default by the VTL operators (e.g. bool_var, errorcode) can be changed in the VTL program, for example through the rename clause. NamePersonalisationSchemeType defines a set of personalisations of VTL standard names that are used in a set of transformations.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:restriction base="VtlDefinitionSchemeType">
				<xs:sequence>
					<xs:element ref="common:Annotations" minOccurs="0"/>
					<xs:element ref="common:Link" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
					<xs:element ref="common:Name" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
					<xs:element ref="common:Description" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
					<xs:sequence minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
						<xs:element ref="NamePersonalisation"/>
					</xs:sequence>
				</xs:sequence>
				<xs:attribute name="urn" type="common:NamePersonalisationSchemeUrnType" use="optional"/>
			</xs:restriction>
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>

	<xs:element name="NamePersonalisation" type="NamePersonalisationType" substitutionGroup="Item">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Deprecated. See NamePersonalisationSchemeType. NamePersonalisation details a name personalisation that is used in a transformation.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>

	<xs:complexType name="NamePersonalisationBaseType" abstract="true">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Deprecated. See NamePersonalisationSchemeType. NamePersonalisationBaseType defines the base refinement of the NamePersonalisationType. Its purpose is to restrict the urn attribute.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:restriction base="UnnestedItemType">
				<xs:sequence>
					<xs:element ref="common:Annotations" minOccurs="0"/>
					<xs:element ref="common:Link" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
					<xs:element ref="common:Name" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
					<xs:element ref="common:Description" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
				</xs:sequence>
				<xs:attribute name="urn" type="common:NamePersonalisationUrnType" use="optional"/>
			</xs:restriction>
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>

	<xs:complexType name="NamePersonalisationType">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Deprecated. See NamePersonalisationSchemeType. NamePersonalisationType defines the structure of a name personalisation. A name personalisation is is used in place of a standard VTL name in some VTL operations.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:extension base="NamePersonalisationBaseType">
				<xs:sequence>
					<xs:element name="VtlDefaultName" type="xs:string">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>Provides the VTL standard name that is being personalised.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
					<xs:element name="PersonalisedName" type="xs:string">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>Provides the personalised name that is used in place of the VTL standard name in the transformation expressions.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
				</xs:sequence>
				<xs:attribute name="vtlArtefact" type="xs:string" use="required">
					<xs:annotation>
						<xs:documentation>Identifies the type of VTL model artefact that is being personalised. In VTL 2.0, this is valuedomain or variable.</xs:documentation>
					</xs:annotation>
				</xs:attribute>
			</xs:extension>
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>

	<xs:complexType name="RulesetSchemeBaseType" abstract="true">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>RulesetSchemeBaseType is an abstract base type for the RulesetSchemeType. It restricts the item types to be only rulesets.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:restriction base="VtlDefinitionSchemeType">
				<xs:sequence>
					<xs:element ref="common:Annotations" minOccurs="0"/>
					<xs:element ref="common:Link" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
					<xs:element ref="common:Name" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
					<xs:element ref="common:Description" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
					<xs:sequence minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
						<xs:element ref="Ruleset"/>
					</xs:sequence>
				</xs:sequence>
				<xs:attribute name="urn" type="common:RulesetSchemeUrnType" use="optional"/>
			</xs:restriction>
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>

	<xs:complexType name="RulesetSchemeType">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>RulesetSchemeType defines a collection of rulesets that are used in transformations, including, from VTL 2.2, the viral propagations.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:extension base="RulesetSchemeBaseType">
				<xs:sequence>
					<xs:element name="VtlMappingScheme" type="common:VtlMappingSchemeReferenceType" minOccurs="0">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>Deprecated. The mappings used by the rulesets are provided by the VTL mapping scheme of the transformation scheme. References a VTL mapping scheme which defines aliases for given SDMX artefacts that are used in the rulesets. Rulesets defined on value domains reference Codelists or Concept Schemes (the latter in VTL are considered as the Value Domains of the variables corresponding to the SDMX Measure Dimensions). The rulesets defined on variables reference Concepts (for which a definite representation is assumed). Therefore, a ruleset should only refer to Codelists, Concept Schemes, and Concepts.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
				</xs:sequence>
			</xs:extension>
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>

	<xs:element name="Ruleset" type="RulesetType" substitutionGroup="Item">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Ruleset details a ruleset within a ruleset scheme.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>

	<xs:complexType name="RulesetBaseType" abstract="true">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>RulesetBaseType defines the base refinement of the RulesetType. Its purpose is to restrict the urn attribute.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:restriction base="UnnestedItemType">
				<xs:sequence>
					<xs:element ref="common:Annotations" minOccurs="0"/>
					<xs:element ref="common:Link" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
					<xs:element ref="common:Name" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
					<xs:element ref="common:Description" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
				</xs:sequence>
				<xs:attribute name="urn" type="common:RulesetUrnType" use="optional"/>
			</xs:restriction>
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>

	<xs:complexType name="RulesetType">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>RulesetType defines the structure of a ruleset. A ruleset is a persistent set of rules which can be invoked by using appropriate VTL operators. From VTL 2.2, a ruleset can also define a viral propagation, i.e. the algorithm for combining the values of a viral attribute, which is not invoked by the operators but applied by all the operators that propagate the viral attributes. An SDMX data attribute is mapped to a viral VTL attribute when a ruleset scheme referenced by the transformation scheme contains a viral propagation for it, defined on its variable (named after its id) or on its value domain (the codelist of its representation).</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:extension base="RulesetBaseType">
				<xs:sequence>
					<xs:element name="RulesetDefinition" type="xs:string">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>A VTL statement for the definition of a ruleset (e.g. define datapoint ruleset, define hierarchical ruleset or, from VTL 2.2, define viral propagation). This must conform to the syntax of the VTL definition language.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
				</xs:sequence>
				<xs:attribute name="rulesetType" type="xs:string" use="required">
					<xs:annotation>
						<xs:documentation>The VTL type of the ruleset. In VTL 2.0, this is datapoint or hierarchical; VTL 2.2 adds viral, for the viral propagations.</xs:documentation>
					</xs:annotation>
				</xs:attribute>
				<xs:attribute name="rulesetScope" type="xs:string" use="required">
					<xs:annotation>
						<xs:documentation>This model artefact on which the ruleset is defined. In VTL 2.0, this is value domain or variable.</xs:documentation>
					</xs:annotation>
				</xs:attribute>
			</xs:extension>
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>

	<xs:complexType name="UserDefinedOperatorSchemeBaseType" abstract="true">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>UserDefinedOperatorSchemeBaseType is an abstract base type for the UserDefinedOperatorSchemeType. It restricts the item types to be only user defined operators.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:restriction base="VtlDefinitionSchemeType">
				<xs:sequence>
					<xs:element ref="common:Annotations" minOccurs="0"/>
					<xs:element ref="common:Link" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
					<xs:element ref="common:Name" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
					<xs:element ref="common:Description" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
					<xs:sequence minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
						<xs:element ref="UserDefinedOperator"/>
					</xs:sequence>
				</xs:sequence>
				<xs:attribute name="urn" type="common:UserDefinedOperatorSchemeUrnType" use="optional"/>
			</xs:restriction>
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>

	<xs:complexType name="UserDefinedOperatorSchemeType">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>UserDefinedOperatorSchemeType defines a collection of user defined operators that are used in transformations.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:extension base="UserDefinedOperatorSchemeBaseType">
				<xs:sequence>
					<xs:element name="VtlMappingScheme" type="common:VtlMappingSchemeReferenceType" minOccurs="0">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>Deprecated. The mappings used by the user defined operators are provided by the VTL mapping scheme of the transformation scheme. References a VTL mapping scheme which defines aliases for given SDMX artefacts that are used in the user defined operators. Although the VTL user defined operators are conceived to be defined on generic operands, so that the specific artefacts to be manipulated are passed as parameters at the invocation, it is also possible that they reference specific SDMX artefacts like Dataflows, Codelists and ConceptSchemes. In this case, the mapping schemes referenced here define the mappings to those artefacts.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
					<xs:element name="RulesetScheme" type="common:RulesetSchemeReferenceType" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>Deprecated. The rulesets used by the user defined operators are provided by the ruleset schemes of the transformation scheme. References a ruleset scheme defining rulesets utilized in the user defined operators.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
				</xs:sequence>
			</xs:extension>
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>

	<xs:element name="UserDefinedOperator" type="UserDefinedOperatorType" substitutionGroup="Item">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>UserDefinedOperator details a user defined operators within a user defined operator scheme.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>	
	</xs:element>

	<xs:complexType name="UserDefinedOperatorBaseType" abstract="true">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>UserDefinedOperatorBaseType defines the base refinement of the UserDefinedOperatorType. Its purpose is to restrict the urn attribute.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:restriction base="UnnestedItemType">
				<xs:sequence>
					<xs:element ref="common:Annotations" minOccurs="0"/>
					<xs:element ref="common:Link" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
					<xs:element ref="common:Name" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
					<xs:element ref="common:Description" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
				</xs:sequence>
				<xs:attribute name="urn" type="common:UserDefinedOperatorUrnType" use="optional"/>
			</xs:restriction>
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>

	<xs:complexType name="UserDefinedOperatorType">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>UserDefinedOperatorType defines the structure of a user defined operator. A user defined operator is a custom VTL operator (not existing in the standard library) that extends the VTL standard library for specific purposes. In addition to its identification and name, and definition of the operator must be provided.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:extension base="UserDefinedOperatorBaseType">
				<xs:sequence>
					<xs:element name="OperatorDefinition" type="xs:string">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>A VTL statement for the definition of a new operator: it specifies the operator name, its parameters and their data types, the VTL expression that defines its behaviour.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
				</xs:sequence>
			</xs:extension>
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>

	<xs:complexType name="CustomTypeSchemeType">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Deprecated. The custom type schemes are deprecated in SDMX 3.2: the default conversions between VTL and SDMX data types apply, unless overridden by the representation defined in the data structure definition of the referenced SDMX artefact; the VTL NULLs become missing values in SDMX, the VTL literals are written in the default format, and explicit type conversions are expressed in the VTL program through the cast operator. CustomTypeSchemeType defines a collection of custom types that are used in transformations.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:restriction base="VtlDefinitionSchemeType">
				<xs:sequence>
					<xs:element ref="common:Annotations" minOccurs="0"/>
					<xs:element ref="common:Link" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
					<xs:element ref="common:Name" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
					<xs:element ref="common:Description" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
					<xs:sequence minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
						<xs:element ref="CustomType"/>
					</xs:sequence>
				</xs:sequence>
				<xs:attribute name="urn" type="common:CustomTypeSchemeUrnType" use="optional"/>
			</xs:restriction>
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>

	<xs:element name="CustomType" type="CustomTypeType" substitutionGroup="Item">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Deprecated. See CustomTypeSchemeType. CustomType details a custom type within a custom type scheme.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>

	<xs:complexType name="CustomTypeBaseType" abstract="true">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Deprecated. See CustomTypeSchemeType. CustomTypeBaseType defines the base refinement of the CustomTypeType. Its purpose is to restrict the urn attribute.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:restriction base="UnnestedItemType">
				<xs:sequence>
					<xs:element ref="common:Annotations" minOccurs="0"/>
					<xs:element ref="common:Link" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
					<xs:element ref="common:Name" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
					<xs:element ref="common:Description" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
				</xs:sequence>
				<xs:attribute name="urn" type="common:CustomTypeUrnType" use="optional"/>
			</xs:restriction>
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>

	<xs:complexType name="CustomTypeType">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Deprecated. See CustomTypeSchemeType. CustomTypeType defines the structure of a custom type. A custom type specifies a custom conversion for a VTL scalar type to a resulting data type. This conversion overrides the default conversion.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:extension base="CustomTypeBaseType">
				<xs:sequence>
					<xs:element name="VtlScalarType" type="xs:string">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>Identifies the VTL scalar type that is to be converted to an resulting output data type.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
					<xs:element name="DataType" type="CustomTypeDataType">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>Identifies the resulting output data type the VTL scalar type is to be converted to. If this is an SDMX data type, it must use the proper SimpleDataType enumeration value. For all other data types, a string value can be used to identify the type.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
					<xs:element name="VtlLiteralFormat" type="xs:string" minOccurs="0">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>The format in which the literals of the VTL scalar type are expressed in the transformations. This is only needed if the format is different than the output format expressed by means of the VTL type.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
					<xs:element name="OutputFormat" type="xs:string" minOccurs="0">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>The format the VTL scalar type has to assume (e.g. YYYY-MM-DD; see VTL specifications), both for the literals in the VTL expressions and for the conversion to the output.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
					<xs:element name="NullValue" type="xs:string" minOccurs="0">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>The value to be produced in the output of the conversion when a component has a null value.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
				</xs:sequence>
			</xs:extension>
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>

	<xs:simpleType name="CustomTypeDataType">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Deprecated. See CustomTypeSchemeType. A simple type that allows for a SDMX simple data type enumeration value or a string value for other data types.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:union memberTypes="common:SimpleDataType xs:string"/>
	</xs:simpleType>

</xs:schema>